WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.964 --> 00:00:04.380
WILLIAM VAMBENEPE:
Good afternoon,

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:06.224
since it's now an
afternoon session.

00:00:06.224 --> 00:00:07.890
That wasn't the
original plan, but we're

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:08.730
glad to have you there.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:09.640
Thank you for coming.

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:10.740
I'm William Vambenepe.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.590
I'm a product manager
and I lead the PM team

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:16.480
for big data services on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:17.762
And with me today is Reza.

00:00:17.762 --> 00:00:18.720
REZA ROKNI: Reza Rokni.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:20.860
I'm part of the Global
Solution Architect team

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:23.400
and I work directly with our
customers, product management,

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.352
and engineering.

00:00:27.282 --> 00:00:28.740
WILLIAM VAMBENEPE:
One way to start

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.600
thinking about the
value of big data

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.770
is just by an example of what
Google did with Street View.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:36.830
You're pretty familiar
with the feature.

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:40.740
A while ago, Google started
to send cars all over cities,

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.740
and then more than
cities, countryside,

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:45.410
and then more than
cars, eventually,

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:49.200
to really take pictures of
many, many parts of the world.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.040
And originally, this was really
just a feature of Google Maps.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.280
The only reason to do that
was that it would be nice,

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.740
when people look at a
destination on Google Maps,

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:00.630
to show them what
the place looks

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:02.250
like that they're looking at.

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:03.900
And so that was a great feature.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:05.110
We needed pictures.

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:08.040
Take the pictures and
show that on the map.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:11.540
What's very interesting
is that, over time,

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:16.180
as technology improved and
as the cost of technology,

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:19.730
especially at scale,
went way lower,

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:23.760
a lot more data was
accessible from those images.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:27.210
And so today, all those
images going back over time

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:31.380
to many, many years, in addition
to being nice to look at,

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:33.540
can be used to extract
data from them.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:37.890
Such as, in this case, the
street number, the signs,

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:40.690
what the display,
what's in the window.

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:43.410
What product is advertised,
at that point of time,

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:45.180
in the window?

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.040
And really, a lot
more information

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.550
that suddenly gives a lot
more perspective on what's

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:52.560
happening in the world.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.740
The reason why I like
this example is not

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:57.690
that-- this is not talk
about Vision API or something

00:01:57.690 --> 00:01:58.860
focused on images.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.710
It really illustrates
the fact that there

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:04.830
is, very often, a lot
more value in the data

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:08.960
than the original purpose for
which the data was collected.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:11.940
And in due time, as the
technology improves,

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:15.090
as the costs go down, as it
becomes easier and easier

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:19.472
for everyone to process data,
that data gets uncovered

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:20.430
and its value realized.

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:23.340
And the fact that you're
here today, that really

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:26.955
puts you in the same
position as the Maps team.

00:02:26.955 --> 00:02:30.420
You now, by virtue of being here
and being in this conference

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.040
and going to the
various sessions,

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:35.850
have access to very
powerful technology

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:38.520
that's much easier to use
and much easier to operate.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:41.490
And so that's what we are
going to discuss today.

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:44.010
Let's start with a few
other applications.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:45.840
Image one is a very
nice one to start

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.120
with because it's very visual.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:51.900
There's many other ways that
data is used productively.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:54.420
The way I like to think
about it is to break it down

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:55.740
in two categories.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:57.890
There is a set of
use cases where

00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:00.410
data is used to inform humans.

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:02.550
A human has to make a decision.

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:04.740
It would be nice if
that decision was

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:07.750
informed by real event data.

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:11.020
And so that's a more
traditional model of analytics,

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:12.390
business intelligence.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:14.550
And that's still very
important, obviously.

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:20.070
And so some examples here where
you can get much more complete

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:23.190
insight into your supply chain,
much more timely insight,

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:23.970
as well.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:27.330
So if one part of your
supply chain, one partner,

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:30.480
is causing issues,
you want to be

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.180
able to know that for
sure, as opposed to having

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:35.490
an intuition that that
partner, really, you get

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:37.380
lots of complaints about them.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:42.270
And getting that insight
quickly when you can act on it.

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:43.920
Another example of
use case, well, I'm

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:48.180
a product manager so I make
product decisions quite often.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:50.250
It's nice when you can
make those decisions based

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:52.290
on actual data, as
opposed to insight.

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:55.200
We all think we
have great intuition

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.120
and we all fool ourselves
when we think that.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:00.900
And so data can be used
to better understand

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:04.110
user behavior, to run
experiments, launch

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:07.380
a feature or a version of
a feature to a set of users

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.990
and really compare how
that affects the behavior.

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:12.660
And so there's just a
few examples of cases

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:16.320
where data is used to inform
humans who make decisions.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:19.350
And then, more and more,
there are cases where

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:21.660
the human is out of the loop.

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:26.250
Where the analysis of
the data is used directly

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:29.620
to affect the behavior
of your applications.

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:32.160
The most typical example
that we see all the time

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:33.690
is when you go
shopping, depending

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:37.020
on what you've bought, what
you purchase, recommendations,

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:39.230
that's well understood.

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:42.900
And there's no human, obviously,
making those recommendations.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:44.100
But it goes way beyond.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.950
There are many
systems where you just

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:49.860
wouldn't have the time for
a human, anyway, to react.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:53.730
When you're looking at financial
transactions happening very

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:55.230
quickly, and time is money.

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:58.140
Fraud has to be
stopped right away.

00:04:58.140 --> 00:04:59.850
It just wouldn't
be possible anyway,

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:02.550
even if you had armies of
humans to do it manually.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:05.500
And that's where you want the
machine to act automatically

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:09.130
or to resolve issues
and automate processes.

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:10.700
These are some of the examples.

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:12.930
Obviously, to make
them happen, you

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:15.370
need to actually
have access to data.

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:17.220
And people sometimes
wonder, well,

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:20.040
do I really have access
to the kind of data?

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:22.421
I don't have cars going
all around the world taking

00:05:22.421 --> 00:05:22.920
pictures.

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:27.840
I don't have that kind of data
which has powerful insights.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.570
And in reality, if
you take a step back

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:32.190
and think about
your data, you need

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:33.930
to think much more broadly.

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:36.290
Obviously, there is the
data that you already

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:37.430
analyzed today.

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:40.170
And that one, you usually
know it quite well

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:43.110
and have a good sense
of the value that

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:44.550
can be extracted from it.

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:47.799
But then, there is a lot
of data that you collect

00:05:47.799 --> 00:05:48.840
but don't really analyze.

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:52.590
Maybe you collected because that
was the default configuration,

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:53.760
to collect all that data.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:56.070
Or you collected
because some people

00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:59.310
want to be able, occasionally,
to go do a point lookup

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:01.620
and find out what happened
that day at that time.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:03.840
Well, let me go in the
log and figure it out.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:06.870
But there is really no
rigorous, holistic use

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:09.540
of the data in that context.

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:13.410
And so that data really is
directly available for more

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:14.820
advanced processing.

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:17.520
And then there's a lot of
data that you could collect

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:20.010
but don't, because if you don't
have a plan to do something

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:21.450
with it, why would you bother?

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:22.680
Why would you collect it?

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:25.480
But as you get access
to the technology,

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:28.140
as that technology
becomes easy to use,

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:31.360
it really makes sense to
start thinking more around,

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:33.060
well, maybe I could
instrument this.

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:35.910
Maybe I could collect
that data and bring it.

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:37.840
And then the fourth
category is data

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:41.430
that you don't necessarily
create and generate,

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:44.040
but that you could get
access to by asking somebody.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:48.030
And so one example would be
data from commercial providers

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:51.780
or other third parties
that you could either have

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:55.506
an arrangement with or purchase,
or data from your partners

00:06:55.506 --> 00:06:56.880
who would be
willing to integrate

00:06:56.880 --> 00:06:57.930
more closely with you.

00:06:57.930 --> 00:07:01.350
So I would encourage you
to think a lot more broadly

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:03.890
about data that's
available on the platform.

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:06.720
One of the things we announced
this morning in the keynote

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:10.050
is that we now have BigQuery
transfer service, which

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:12.450
is going to automate
bringing your marketing

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:15.670
data at Google from AdWord,
from DoubleClick, from YouTube,

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:18.780
if you're a publisher,
directly into Cloud.

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:21.090
We also work with commercial
dataset providers,

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:23.160
like Dow Jones, to make
data available directly.

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:27.440
So there's really a big universe
of data available to you.

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:30.150
Now on the downside,
historically, it's

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:32.760
been hard to use
data, which is why

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:34.720
many people haven't done it.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:37.480
Or those who've done it,
installed Hadoop Cluster

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:40.500
and tried to get
some value there

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:44.250
have often found
that pretty painful.

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:46.710
And one of the points
that has made that painful

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:50.340
is that you had to--
that data rarely comes

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:52.620
in a way that's ready to
use and ready to process.

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:54.900
You have to prepare it,
you have to clean it,

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:56.670
you have to get it
in the right shape,

00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:57.960
and that's been challenging.

00:07:57.960 --> 00:07:59.850
And so one thing
that we introduced

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:03.030
this morning is a new
service on Google Cloud

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:06.390
called Google Cloud Dataprep,
specifically to address that.

00:08:06.390 --> 00:08:08.370
And to address
that in a way which

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:12.017
does not require writing
code and having access

00:08:12.017 --> 00:08:12.600
to developers.

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:16.050
So it's empowering the end
users of the data, those

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:18.870
who understand the model,
those who understand

00:08:18.870 --> 00:08:22.860
the content of the data, to
help themselves and prepare it

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:24.060
for analysis.

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:27.270
And so rather than waving
my hands and describing it,

00:08:27.270 --> 00:08:30.750
I'm going to do that via
an example and a demo.

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:33.840
Let me set the example.

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:36.390
The use case here is
you are a retailer

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:38.080
and you have transaction data.

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:40.740
So every time somebody
does a purchase,

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:43.110
that's a new row
on that transaction

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:46.410
table at the top, which
contains, among other things,

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:48.512
all the products
that were purchased.

00:08:48.512 --> 00:08:49.470
And so you get one row.

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:53.310
If you buy five products,
it's one transaction, one row.

00:08:53.310 --> 00:08:55.500
And then, within that
row, you see that there

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:57.510
is that array of JSON objects.

00:08:57.510 --> 00:09:01.710
And so each product
purchased was one JSON object

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:05.280
about that product
altogether in that array.

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:06.000
So that's nice.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:08.310
You have all your data.

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:10.410
But here, what we're
going to try to do

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:15.030
is to map that product purchase
to an ad impression that

00:09:15.030 --> 00:09:16.330
may have influenced it.

00:09:16.330 --> 00:09:20.820
And so the second dataset
is ad impressions.

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:24.469
What ad, about what product,
had been displayed to what user?

00:09:24.469 --> 00:09:26.010
So what we're going
to try to do here

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:31.560
is simply let me see what are
the instances where a given

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:34.500
customer purchased
a given product

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:37.260
after that same customer
has been shown an ad

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:39.300
for that product?

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:40.350
It's not rocket science.

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:43.020
It's something any
business person

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:44.520
should be able to express.

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:47.715
But in practice, when
your data looks like that,

00:09:47.715 --> 00:09:50.110
there is quite a bit of work
to be able to extract that.

00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:53.970
Because in my retail
data site, the product ID

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:57.960
is buried within all
the product purchased

00:09:57.960 --> 00:09:59.550
in that one transaction.

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:01.970
And then in the
advertising that dataset,

00:10:01.970 --> 00:10:05.070
the product ID is a
parameter in the URL.

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:08.430
So normally, I would have
to write quite a bit of code

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:11.760
to go and extradite data and
do all the cleaning needed.

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:14.130
And so if we can switch
to the demo screen,

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:16.810
we are now going to show how
to do that in Google Cloud Data

00:10:16.810 --> 00:10:17.310
Prep.

00:10:20.070 --> 00:10:22.097
Can I have the demo
laptop on, please?

00:10:22.097 --> 00:10:22.596
OK.

00:10:22.596 --> 00:10:23.370
We're on?

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:24.180
Yes.

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:26.550
This is the empty UI.

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:27.577
Nothing has been loaded.

00:10:27.577 --> 00:10:29.910
The first thing that we're
going to do is create a flow.

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:33.180
A flow is really an
organizational envelope

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:36.190
for a set of work you're
trying to purchase.

00:10:36.190 --> 00:10:38.010
So we're going to
create this new flow.

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:41.310
And the first thing we do
is to add datasets to it.

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:43.560
These are the two
datasets I mentioned.

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:46.560
This is a CSV file in
Google Cloud Storage.

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:48.360
These are my ad impressions.

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:50.460
And this one is
a BigQuery table,

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:53.910
which contains my
retail transactions.

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:55.440
So I just have to add them.

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:59.090
And then the tool will
automatically load these two.

00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:00.690
In the case of the
BigQuery table,

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:03.510
it can show me a
preview of that table.

00:11:03.510 --> 00:11:06.810
The CSV file, well, it's just a
text file so it's pretty messy.

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:10.560
But already the tool
infers some transformation

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:13.290
to break it down
by row, by column,

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:18.400
and to discover that
the first row contains

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:19.530
the name of the headers.

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:21.450
And so already,
there is a recipe

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:25.710
that has been auto-created with
some simple transformation.

00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:27.200
Now let's make use of that.

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:30.020
We're going to start
with the BigQuery table

00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:31.590
and we're going to
add a new recipe.

00:11:31.590 --> 00:11:36.060
And so a recipe is the way
that [? data ?] prep works.

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:38.400
In effect, what you do when
you work with the tool is

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:41.430
you create recipes
that capture the way

00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:43.260
you want to prepare the data.

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:46.320
Here's the interface where
you spend most of your time.

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:50.750
What it lets you do is interact
with your data directly.

00:11:50.750 --> 00:11:54.720
It's not a tool when you have to
think about the transformation

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:58.620
you want to apply and then
drag and drop components that

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:00.370
represent that transformation.

00:12:00.370 --> 00:12:03.030
No, you think starting
from the data, as opposed

00:12:03.030 --> 00:12:05.170
to from the transformation.

00:12:05.170 --> 00:12:08.340
And so in this
case, you recognize

00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:10.640
what I was showing on
the slide of that--

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:13.170
let me just expand the
column a little bit.

00:12:13.170 --> 00:12:16.360
That array of JSON object,
which is pretty messy.

00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:20.010
If I expand one of them,
there's quite a lot there.

00:12:20.010 --> 00:12:23.250
And so to try to make sense
of this, all I have to do

00:12:23.250 --> 00:12:24.657
is click on that column.

00:12:24.657 --> 00:12:26.490
And that's really the
principle of the tool.

00:12:26.490 --> 00:12:30.150
In practice, the way it works
is you click on the data

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:33.180
that you're interested
in and you indicate,

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:35.730
via those clicks, what
you're trying to get done.

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:37.740
And then at the
bottom, you can see

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:41.190
the tool suggests a
potential transformation that

00:12:41.190 --> 00:12:44.460
would make sense for that data.

00:12:44.460 --> 00:12:47.280
In this case, I see there's
one suggestion here, which

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:50.190
is convert this
one row which has

00:12:50.190 --> 00:12:52.950
an array into a set of rows.

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:55.880
And so each item in the
array becomes its own row.

00:12:55.880 --> 00:12:59.685
And so if I look at
what this one would do,

00:12:59.685 --> 00:13:01.350
if I go into preview--

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:02.880
right now I'm in
the preview and it

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:05.520
shows me that-- let me
just close this here.

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:07.950
It shows me that
now, you see where

00:13:07.950 --> 00:13:10.580
I used to have an
array of JSON objects,

00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:12.070
now I have one JSON object.

00:13:12.070 --> 00:13:15.750
Because what used to be
one row per transaction

00:13:15.750 --> 00:13:19.440
is now one row per product
purchased in the transaction.

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:21.690
So if I bought five
products, I now

00:13:21.690 --> 00:13:24.750
have five individual rows, which
is more convenient because I'm

00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:26.970
looking for a specific product.

00:13:26.970 --> 00:13:30.660
And so I like that and I'm
going to add to recipe.

00:13:30.660 --> 00:13:35.779
And so now, here are my rows
already there in the recipe.

00:13:35.779 --> 00:13:37.320
The next thing I
want to do is I want

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:40.355
to go inside that JSON object
and extract, specifically,

00:13:40.355 --> 00:13:42.000
the product key.

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:43.680
Now, there are many
ways to do that.

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:46.260
Because again, the tool is going
to try to guess and help me.

00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:49.230
For example, I can
click on the column

00:13:49.230 --> 00:13:51.690
again and then look
at a suggestion.

00:13:51.690 --> 00:13:57.780
The first suggestion is to
create one column per variable

00:13:57.780 --> 00:13:58.350
in JSON.

00:13:58.350 --> 00:14:00.960
So I have 13 variables,
[? cells, ?] [INAUDIBLE],

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:02.640
line key item, product key.

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:05.400
I could just say,
expand that and create

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:06.937
13 different columns.

00:14:06.937 --> 00:14:08.520
And that would be a
good way to do it.

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:12.480
In this case, I actually only
care about the product key.

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:16.530
So another way I could do that
is I can just go and highlight

00:14:16.530 --> 00:14:18.870
Product Key and that's my hint.

00:14:18.870 --> 00:14:21.234
And then the tool is
going to think about that.

00:14:21.234 --> 00:14:22.650
And then the
suggestion is, do you

00:14:22.650 --> 00:14:25.350
want to create a new column
using that product key

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:25.980
variable?

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:27.210
And that sounds good to me.

00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:29.380
The preview looks like
what I want to do.

00:14:29.380 --> 00:14:31.290
I want to extract
that product key.

00:14:31.290 --> 00:14:33.824
And so all I do is
add that to recipe.

00:14:33.824 --> 00:14:35.490
If I can close that
little preview here.

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:39.170
And then-- sorry, wrong click.

00:14:39.170 --> 00:14:42.570
Here I have that new column that
extracts the product key, now

00:14:42.570 --> 00:14:46.470
easily available without
having to write any code.

00:14:46.470 --> 00:14:48.930
Let's switch to my other
dataset, the advertising

00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:49.620
dataset.

00:14:49.620 --> 00:14:52.930
Remember, that's a CSV file.

00:14:52.930 --> 00:14:55.280
And already, as
I showed earlier,

00:14:55.280 --> 00:14:58.960
the tool has created some steps
in the recipe to break down

00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:01.410
or to use the new line
as a row separator,

00:15:01.410 --> 00:15:04.260
to recognize the column
separator, and the header.

00:15:04.260 --> 00:15:08.040
And those steps sound good,
so I'm going to keep them.

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:11.460
If I look at the histogram
of the data for every column,

00:15:11.460 --> 00:15:13.770
I notice that, for
the event time, which

00:15:13.770 --> 00:15:16.860
is when the ad was displayed,
there is some red here.

00:15:16.860 --> 00:15:19.810
And the red represents
mismatched values.

00:15:19.810 --> 00:15:22.110
And so again, I
just click on that.

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:24.000
And then the tool asks
me, what do you want?

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:26.690
Do you want to only
keep those values

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:28.380
or do you want to
delete those values?

00:15:28.380 --> 00:15:31.440
And in this case,
obviously, what

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:34.200
makes sense here is to delete
the values because this is not

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:35.130
useful to me.

00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:39.000
And so I just select that
choice, add to recipe.

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:41.760
And now we can see that those
mismatched values are filtered

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:44.550
out and I can keep working on.

00:15:44.550 --> 00:15:46.580
Let's keep looking
at the histograms.

00:15:46.580 --> 00:15:48.720
For User ID, for
