00:16:26.010 --> 00:16:29.400
And so that's why that user
is so predominant in my logs,

00:16:29.400 --> 00:16:31.860
is that probably
it's a robot that's

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:33.040
just clicking every second.

00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:34.720
So I'm not going to keep it.

00:16:34.720 --> 00:16:35.760
I'm doing the opposite.

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:37.660
I'm going to delete that user.

00:16:37.660 --> 00:16:39.840
So now it shows me,
in the red, those rows

00:16:39.840 --> 00:16:41.310
are going to be removed.

00:16:41.310 --> 00:16:42.930
Yes, I like that.

00:16:42.930 --> 00:16:44.080
I add this to the recipe.

00:16:44.080 --> 00:16:44.580
OK.

00:16:44.580 --> 00:16:48.000
We've done some cleanup
removing invalid time values,

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:51.090
removing suspicious users.

00:16:51.090 --> 00:16:53.610
Let's go back to the
original goal, which

00:16:53.610 --> 00:16:56.220
is to extract the product ID.

00:16:56.220 --> 00:17:01.460
And as you remember, this is the
URL for the ad and the product

00:17:01.460 --> 00:17:02.940
ID is embedded there.

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:07.950
So really, all I have to do is
to click to expand one of them

00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:11.730
and just give the tool a hint,
like I'm looking for this.

00:17:11.730 --> 00:17:13.109
And so first hint.

00:17:13.109 --> 00:17:14.849
And the tool thinks,
oh, are you looking

00:17:14.849 --> 00:17:16.740
for that specific value?

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:17.369
Well, no.

00:17:17.369 --> 00:17:18.660
I'm looking for a pattern here.

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:20.579
So let me give it
the second hint.

00:17:20.579 --> 00:17:22.020
I'm going to close this one.

00:17:22.020 --> 00:17:23.339
Well, it's not just this one.

00:17:23.339 --> 00:17:25.859
Let me open another
one and let me select

00:17:25.859 --> 00:17:27.839
that pattern on another one.

00:17:27.839 --> 00:17:28.920
Now, it's like, oh, OK.

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:29.430
I get it.

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:30.750
You're not looking
for that value.

00:17:30.750 --> 00:17:32.120
You're looking for that pattern.

00:17:32.120 --> 00:17:35.760
And it's making suggestions
as in where I would want

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:37.950
to be looking for that pattern.

00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:40.320
And so here, for
example, it's digit

00:17:40.320 --> 00:17:42.600
followed by dash
followed by digits

00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:44.190
starting after the equals sign.

00:17:44.190 --> 00:17:45.070
OK.

00:17:45.070 --> 00:17:46.020
That's a good pattern.

00:17:46.020 --> 00:17:48.603
Actually, I'm going to make it
more robust because there could

00:17:48.603 --> 00:17:50.100
be other-- have more variables.

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:53.100
There could be other
equal signs in my URL.

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:56.430
So I'm going to
modify the recipe.

00:17:56.430 --> 00:17:58.530
And instead of
starting after equal,

00:17:58.530 --> 00:18:00.870
I'm going to have it
start after PID equal.

00:18:00.870 --> 00:18:03.330
This way, I'll be more
robust if there is

00:18:03.330 --> 00:18:05.950
more patterns, more variables.

00:18:05.950 --> 00:18:07.920
Add that to the recipe.

00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:08.790
Here it is.

00:18:08.790 --> 00:18:11.070
It created this
new column, URL1.

00:18:11.070 --> 00:18:13.810
It just adds a 1 to
the original one,

00:18:13.810 --> 00:18:17.490
which contains those product
IDs that have been extracted.

00:18:17.490 --> 00:18:23.190
Let's rename it to something
a bit more useful than URL1.

00:18:23.190 --> 00:18:27.670
This is the product ID.

00:18:27.670 --> 00:18:30.910
And then the renaming, that's
another step in the recipe.

00:18:30.910 --> 00:18:33.170
And you don't have to worry
about optimizing the steps

00:18:33.170 --> 00:18:36.070
and doing the filtering
before the renaming.

00:18:36.070 --> 00:18:38.227
All that will be optimized
automatically for you.

00:18:38.227 --> 00:18:39.310
So don't worry about that.

00:18:39.310 --> 00:18:42.690
Just express what
you want to get done.

00:18:42.690 --> 00:18:44.410
And now, we have
those two datasets.

00:18:44.410 --> 00:18:45.490
We've cleaned them.

00:18:45.490 --> 00:18:47.980
We've extracted the product ID.

00:18:47.980 --> 00:18:50.020
Now we can join between them.

00:18:50.020 --> 00:18:53.310
And so let's do that
directly in the tool.

00:18:53.310 --> 00:18:57.180
There's a join
that you can apply.

00:18:57.180 --> 00:18:59.040
Actually, let me-- yes.

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:03.270
Now it's asking me,
again, what I want

00:19:03.270 --> 00:19:06.380
to join that current dataset.

00:19:06.380 --> 00:19:10.560
So I'm going to join it against
the retail dataset, which

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:13.300
I've prepared.

00:19:13.300 --> 00:19:14.840
Select the preview.

00:19:14.840 --> 00:19:16.570
Let's pick the join keys.

00:19:16.570 --> 00:19:19.690
It's going to look at my data
and try to infer the join key.

00:19:19.690 --> 00:19:23.590
And what it suggests is to
join product ID from the ads

00:19:23.590 --> 00:19:28.040
to product key from the
transaction, and that's one.

00:19:28.040 --> 00:19:28.790
That's a good one.

00:19:28.790 --> 00:19:29.740
I like that one.

00:19:29.740 --> 00:19:34.750
But remember, I'm trying to
look for a purchase of a product

00:19:34.750 --> 00:19:37.810
by a given user if
that same user has

00:19:37.810 --> 00:19:39.080
seen the ad for that product.

00:19:39.080 --> 00:19:44.020
So I also have to add an
additional set of join keys

00:19:44.020 --> 00:19:45.340
to do on the user.

00:19:45.340 --> 00:19:48.080
And so it's suggesting
to join the user ID.

00:19:48.080 --> 00:19:48.580
OK.

00:19:48.580 --> 00:19:49.480
That sounds good.

00:19:49.480 --> 00:19:52.330
But user ID with product
key, I do not want this.

00:19:52.330 --> 00:19:54.280
So I can edit.

00:19:54.280 --> 00:19:57.580
And what I'm going to join
with is the customer key.

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:02.550
Here, we have two pairs of keys.

00:20:02.550 --> 00:20:06.660
Product ID to product key,
user ID to customer key.

00:20:06.660 --> 00:20:07.830
That's what I want.

00:20:07.830 --> 00:20:10.890
I can select which
columns I want to keep

00:20:10.890 --> 00:20:12.960
in the result of my join.

00:20:12.960 --> 00:20:15.510
Here, I want to compare the
time difference between the two.

00:20:15.510 --> 00:20:19.909
I want to pick the event time
and I want to pick the order--

00:20:19.909 --> 00:20:20.700
where is the order?

00:20:20.700 --> 00:20:22.290
Order date, there it is.

00:20:22.290 --> 00:20:25.470
And I can pick any
other I want, obviously.

00:20:25.470 --> 00:20:27.630
And then add that
join to the recipe.

00:20:27.630 --> 00:20:32.190
And here I am, back
in the data center QI

00:20:32.190 --> 00:20:36.920
to show me the result of
that join across those keys

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:38.340
and with the time.

00:20:38.340 --> 00:20:41.420
In many cases, maybe that's the
kind of cleaning I want to do.

00:20:41.420 --> 00:20:42.479
I'm done here.

00:20:42.479 --> 00:20:44.520
I don't have to do the
difference between the two

00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:49.110
times because I will do that
later in my visualization tool.

00:20:49.110 --> 00:20:50.550
Or maybe I want to do it here.

00:20:50.550 --> 00:20:51.810
Maybe I want to add some more.

00:20:51.810 --> 00:20:54.480
And so I can select
the event time.

00:20:54.480 --> 00:20:56.650
And then I select--

00:20:56.650 --> 00:20:59.160
where's the other one?

00:20:59.160 --> 00:21:01.107
The order time.

00:21:01.107 --> 00:21:02.440
What happened to the order time?

00:21:02.440 --> 00:21:05.610
There it is, the order date.

00:21:05.610 --> 00:21:09.120
Now, if I select those
two, what the tool suggests

00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:11.641
me is to create a new column.

00:21:11.641 --> 00:21:12.140
Yes.

00:21:12.140 --> 00:21:14.480
Except I want the
time difference.

00:21:14.480 --> 00:21:15.200
There it is.

00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:17.700
Here, the tool suggests
to create a new column

00:21:17.700 --> 00:21:22.052
where the value is the time
difference between those two

00:21:22.052 --> 00:21:23.310
[? dead ?] columns.

00:21:23.310 --> 00:21:26.120
I like that, except seconds
is probably too granular,

00:21:26.120 --> 00:21:28.010
so I'm going to modify.

00:21:28.010 --> 00:21:29.690
And instead of
seconds, I'm going

00:21:29.690 --> 00:21:32.246
to do it expressed in minutes.

00:21:32.246 --> 00:21:35.220
Add that to recipe.

00:21:35.220 --> 00:21:36.180
And oops, sorry.

00:21:36.180 --> 00:21:39.110
Wrong click again.

00:21:39.110 --> 00:21:39.610
There we go.

00:21:39.610 --> 00:21:41.260
We have that new column created.

00:21:41.260 --> 00:21:43.960
That content is the
result of the calculation.

00:21:43.960 --> 00:21:46.180
Let's rename it
something more useful.

00:21:46.180 --> 00:21:51.480
We're going to call
it time difference.

00:21:51.480 --> 00:21:53.270
Add to recipe.

00:21:53.270 --> 00:21:55.393
Again, here-- that's about it.

00:22:00.794 --> 00:22:02.705
[INAUDIBLE] Did I click
in the wrong place?

00:22:02.705 --> 00:22:03.330
Oh, here it is.

00:22:03.330 --> 00:22:05.520
It was already added
so clicked it twice.

00:22:05.520 --> 00:22:07.510
I have now that
new column that's

00:22:07.510 --> 00:22:09.795
added to do the time difference.

00:22:09.795 --> 00:22:12.660
As you can see, without
writing a line of code,

00:22:12.660 --> 00:22:14.250
just by interacting
with my data,

00:22:14.250 --> 00:22:18.720
I'm pointing at things I
want to do, choosing columns

00:22:18.720 --> 00:22:21.990
I want to use together, I was
able to describe that pipeline

00:22:21.990 --> 00:22:23.310
which I can then execute.

00:22:23.310 --> 00:22:26.839
And we'll talk about Dataflow,
the underlying service.

00:22:26.839 --> 00:22:28.380
But the thing to
know in that context

00:22:28.380 --> 00:22:30.010
is that your job is done.

00:22:30.010 --> 00:22:32.760
The execution is, at that
point, fully automated.

00:22:32.760 --> 00:22:34.170
You just have to run the job.

00:22:34.170 --> 00:22:36.750
There is nothing to
deploy, nothing to manage.

00:22:36.750 --> 00:22:40.380
Even if your retail transaction
is terabytes and terabytes

00:22:40.380 --> 00:22:42.990
in size, it's going to
run in Google Cloud,

00:22:42.990 --> 00:22:47.024
execute, and deliver to you the
result of that transformation.

00:22:47.024 --> 00:22:48.690
Let's go back to the
slides now, please.

00:22:52.270 --> 00:22:54.550
That was a demo of how,
without writing code,

00:22:54.550 --> 00:22:59.590
you can do a lot of very
typical, common, and useful

00:22:59.590 --> 00:23:00.790
transformational steps.

00:23:00.790 --> 00:23:04.510
Now, there are cases where
this is not the right tool.

00:23:04.510 --> 00:23:07.150
There are a lot
of more advanced,

00:23:07.150 --> 00:23:09.640
with pretty complex
processing algorithms,

00:23:09.640 --> 00:23:13.150
with branching logic, where,
really, code is the right way

00:23:13.150 --> 00:23:14.810
to express your transformation.

00:23:14.810 --> 00:23:18.010
Maybe you want to run
a streaming pipeline.

00:23:18.010 --> 00:23:20.650
In that case, you
want to write code

00:23:20.650 --> 00:23:23.020
because code is the best
way to express that.

00:23:23.020 --> 00:23:25.150
But you still want to
do that in a way that's

00:23:25.150 --> 00:23:26.680
friendly and productive.

00:23:26.680 --> 00:23:29.740
And historically, that
hasn't been the case.

00:23:29.740 --> 00:23:32.820
Historically, a lot of
large scale data processing,

00:23:32.820 --> 00:23:35.440
the tools available
have been designed

00:23:35.440 --> 00:23:37.930
for the convenience
of infrastructure

00:23:37.930 --> 00:23:39.540
and not of the user.

00:23:39.540 --> 00:23:41.320
Take MapReduce, for example.

00:23:41.320 --> 00:23:42.550
MapReduce is awesome.

00:23:42.550 --> 00:23:44.550
Google was built on MapReduce.

00:23:44.550 --> 00:23:48.070
But really, MapReduce
forces a developer

00:23:48.070 --> 00:23:50.630
to learn a new way of thinking.

00:23:50.630 --> 00:23:53.740
And especially if they have
a more complex pipeline

00:23:53.740 --> 00:23:54.910
they have to optimize.

00:23:54.910 --> 00:23:59.200
It's actually a lot of work to
write really good MapReduce.

00:23:59.200 --> 00:24:01.180
But it's very easy
then, once you've

00:24:01.180 --> 00:24:03.250
done it in MapReduce,
for the infrastructure

00:24:03.250 --> 00:24:04.420
to paralyze your work.

00:24:04.420 --> 00:24:06.330
So that was the trade-off.

00:24:06.330 --> 00:24:08.990
But that has limited access
to be big data to a much

00:24:08.990 --> 00:24:11.290
smaller subset of developers.

00:24:11.290 --> 00:24:13.460
And that really doesn't
have to be anymore.

00:24:13.460 --> 00:24:14.650
Same thing with batch.

00:24:14.650 --> 00:24:16.614
For some reason,
as an industry, we

00:24:16.614 --> 00:24:19.030
tend to assume that there's
something natural about batch.

00:24:19.030 --> 00:24:22.210
That, of course, things
at scale happen in batch.

00:24:22.210 --> 00:24:24.940
And in reality, there's really
nothing intuitive about that.

00:24:24.940 --> 00:24:28.060
It's just the force of habit
that makes us think about that.

00:24:28.060 --> 00:24:30.540
The world doesn't work in batch.

00:24:30.540 --> 00:24:31.840
Things happen continuously.

00:24:31.840 --> 00:24:34.930
And so the tools have been
forcing us to think in batch

00:24:34.930 --> 00:24:37.540
mode when, really, we shouldn't.

00:24:37.540 --> 00:24:41.590
Also, they've been forcing
us to think about events,

00:24:41.590 --> 00:24:44.650
in terms of when they're
processed, when they arrive

00:24:44.650 --> 00:24:48.310
in your pricing
logic, as opposed

00:24:48.310 --> 00:24:50.950
to when they actually happened,
which is not always the same.

00:24:50.950 --> 00:24:54.280
And so the reason
why, right now, there

00:24:54.280 --> 00:24:57.070
is a lot more easy
access to big data

00:24:57.070 --> 00:24:59.590
is because those
constraints have really

00:24:59.590 --> 00:25:03.250
been removed by a much more
mature programming model.

00:25:03.250 --> 00:25:05.590
And Reza is going to
tell us about that.

00:25:05.590 --> 00:25:06.923
REZA ROKNI: Thank you very much.

00:25:08.745 --> 00:25:10.120
The last couple
of years, I think

00:25:10.120 --> 00:25:12.220
I've been very, very lucky
in that I've actually worked

00:25:12.220 --> 00:25:14.770
directly with our customers
doing a little bit of that code

00:25:14.770 --> 00:25:18.040
stuff to actually convert
batch processing pipelines over

00:25:18.040 --> 00:25:19.360
to streaming.

00:25:19.360 --> 00:25:22.170
And actually, before we
talk about Dataflow itself,

00:25:22.170 --> 00:25:25.170
let's think about how we're
going to collect this data.

00:25:25.170 --> 00:25:27.790
Let's just take two simple
examples of stream data.

00:25:27.790 --> 00:25:29.410
One is IoT data.

00:25:29.410 --> 00:25:32.530
Imagine there's lots of
factories that we have.

00:25:32.530 --> 00:25:34.705
There is IoT devices
measuring temperature,

00:25:34.705 --> 00:25:36.580
measuring the number of
machines that are on.

00:25:36.580 --> 00:25:41.037
All of these devices are sending
information to somewhere.

00:25:41.037 --> 00:25:43.120
And the other use case
that I commonly come across

00:25:43.120 --> 00:25:44.650
is click stream.

00:25:44.650 --> 00:25:47.597
As users are clicking around
your applications on a website,

00:25:47.597 --> 00:25:49.930
they're doing stuff on their
mobile, all of these events

00:25:49.930 --> 00:25:52.429
are click stream information
that's being recorded and being

00:25:52.429 --> 00:25:53.040
processed.

00:25:53.040 --> 00:25:54.280
Normally, obviously,
that usually

00:25:54.280 --> 00:25:55.738
gets processed by
batch processing,

00:25:55.738 --> 00:25:57.580
but as we move to
stream processing,

00:25:57.580 --> 00:25:59.350
we now need a way
of capturing that.

00:25:59.350 --> 00:26:04.640
And Google Cloud Pub/Sub API
is a publish and subscribe API.

00:26:04.640 --> 00:26:07.390
There's no running up machines.

00:26:07.390 --> 00:26:08.890
It's fully managed service.

00:26:08.890 --> 00:26:11.140
Essentially, it's the
easiest part of any POC

00:26:11.140 --> 00:26:12.370
that I do with customers.

00:26:12.370 --> 00:26:13.420
It's four or five clicks.

00:26:13.420 --> 00:26:14.437
I get a topic created.

00:26:14.437 --> 00:26:17.020
And what that allows you to do
is, from anywhere in the globe,

00:26:17.020 --> 00:26:18.550
you can send information to it.

00:26:18.550 --> 00:26:20.800
We can process it in
any region that we like.

00:26:20.800 --> 00:26:22.670
It is highly
available and it means

00:26:22.670 --> 00:26:26.170
when a message is recorded
and acknowledged by Pub/Sub,

00:26:26.170 --> 00:26:29.080
it will actually keep it in the
system for up to seven days,

00:26:29.080 --> 00:26:31.660
or until the subscription
pulls that message

00:26:31.660 --> 00:26:33.737
and acknowledges that
it's being processed.

00:26:33.737 --> 00:26:35.320
This piece usually
takes a few minutes

00:26:35.320 --> 00:26:38.395
to set up in any POC and it
will deal with several messages

00:26:38.395 --> 00:26:40.240
a second or, as with
some of my customers,

00:26:40.240 --> 00:26:43.260
hundreds of thousands
of messages per second.

00:26:43.260 --> 00:26:47.580
Going on to Dataflow
itself, historically,

00:26:47.580 --> 00:26:50.880
it's been considered that if
you wanted accurate processing,

00:26:50.880 --> 00:26:53.410
you tended to need a
batch processing pipeline.

00:26:53.410 --> 00:26:55.290
So you would have
batch technology,

00:26:55.290 --> 00:26:57.630
you would collect all the
files at the end of the day,

00:26:57.630 --> 00:26:59.190
maybe do some
corrections, some ETL,

00:26:59.190 --> 00:27:01.381
and then do some batch
processing on it.

00:27:01.381 --> 00:27:03.630
If you wanted low latency,
you would then go and build

00:27:03.630 --> 00:27:05.940
a completely separate
technology stack

00:27:05.940 --> 00:27:07.980
that would absorb
the streams of data

00:27:07.980 --> 00:27:11.614
and do some processing for real
time dashboards, et cetera.

00:27:11.614 --> 00:27:13.530
But it was always
considered that that was not

00:27:13.530 --> 00:27:19.580
accurate enough for something
like a billing pipeline.

00:27:19.580 --> 00:27:22.460
Now, why is streaming difficult?

00:27:22.460 --> 00:27:25.630
Well, let's take that simple
example of click stream.

00:27:25.630 --> 00:27:28.490
Now, one of the common
problems of streaming pipelines

00:27:28.490 --> 00:27:30.110
is late data.

00:27:30.110 --> 00:27:32.320
Imagine one of your
applications is a mobile device.

00:27:32.320 --> 00:27:35.420
Your users are going to be
going in and out of cell range,

00:27:35.420 --> 00:27:36.900
they're going to
lose connectivity.

00:27:36.900 --> 00:27:39.260
They're still using,
potentially, the application.

00:27:39.260 --> 00:27:41.810
As it's recording information,
when it suddenly comes back

00:27:41.810 --> 00:27:43.997
online, its going to
send a clump of data.

00:27:43.997 --> 00:27:45.830
Now, if one of the
things that we want to do

00:27:45.830 --> 00:27:47.600
is accurately count
how many people

00:27:47.600 --> 00:27:50.144
are on our applications
any point in that day,

00:27:50.144 --> 00:27:51.560
at this point,
we've got a problem

00:27:51.560 --> 00:27:53.090
because we're going to miscount.

00:27:53.090 --> 00:27:54.710
Now, what Dataflow
allows us to do

00:27:54.710 --> 00:27:57.590
is it gives us a powerful
programming model that

00:27:57.590 --> 00:27:59.740
is geared towards streaming.

00:27:59.740 --> 00:28:01.110
It can deal with batch, as well.

00:28:01.110 --> 00:28:03.860
But what it allows
you to do is deal

00:28:03.860 --> 00:28:05.120
with things like late data.

00:28:05.120 --> 00:28:07.250
It gives you simple
primitives to deal

00:28:07.250 --> 00:28:10.520
with things like windowing
and sessionization.

00:28:10.520 --> 00:28:13.520
And the Dataflow
programming model in SDK

00:28:13.520 --> 00:28:15.170
was contributed to Apache Beam.

00:28:15.170 --> 00:28:17.500
It's now an open source project.

00:28:17.500 --> 00:28:20.180
And Apache Beam, there
are several folks

00:28:20.180 --> 00:28:21.950
building runners for it.

00:28:21.950 --> 00:28:24.860
So we have Spark and also
other execution engines

00:28:24.860 --> 00:28:27.380
like Flink and Apex who
are building runners.

00:28:27.380 --> 00:28:28.910
Now very quickly,
I'm going to show

00:28:28.910 --> 00:28:31.800
a demo of a Dataflow running.

00:28:31.800 --> 00:28:34.490
So if we can move over
to the laptop, please.

00:28:34.490 --> 00:28:37.010
Thank you very much.

00:28:37.010 --> 00:28:40.250
Right now, we're connecting to
the monitoring UI of Dataflow.

00:28:40.250 --> 00:28:41.960
And the example
that's working here,

00:28:41.960 --> 00:28:43.850
for anyone who was at
the session yesterday,

00:28:43.850 --> 00:28:45.830
this is an IoT example.

00:28:45.830 --> 00:28:48.530
There is messages
coming from a factory.

00:28:48.530 --> 00:28:51.860
They have got temperature,
et cetera, encoded in them.

00:28:51.860 --> 00:28:54.410
What we're doing is we're
absorbing that through Pub/Sub

00:28:54.410 --> 00:28:56.780
and Dataflow is
pulling from Pub/Sub.

00:28:56.780 --> 00:28:59.720
It's reading the
IoT device data.

00:28:59.720 --> 00:29:00.857
We are parsing the message.

00:29:00.857 --> 00:29:02.690
We're pulling out little
bits of information

00:29:02.690 --> 00:29:04.910
from the JSON
package that's there.

00:29:04.910 --> 00:29:07.220
And then, I'm actually
doing three different types

00:29:07.220 --> 00:29:09.590
of processing with that data.

00:29:09.590 --> 00:29:12.800
The first one, and I consider
this, best practice always,

00:29:12.800 --> 00:29:15.560
is before any processing,
I want all the raw data

00:29:15.560 --> 00:29:17.300
to be dumped into a repository.

00:29:17.300 --> 00:29:20.000
And at this point, we're
putting it into BigQuery

00:29:20.000 --> 00:29:22.240
because then we can run
SQL analysis against it.

00:29:22.240 --> 00:29:24.830
We'll come back to
BigQuery in a moment.

00:29:24.830 --> 00:29:29.510
And on the other side, we are
doing some basic processing

00:29:29.510 --> 00:29:30.654
against the data.

00:29:30.654 --> 00:29:32.570
So here, I'm creating a
simple sliding window.

00:29:32.570 --> 00:29:33.920
That's two lines of code.

00:29:33.920 --> 00:29:35.720
Then I'm doing a
mean calculation,

00:29:35.720 --> 00:29:38.540
so within the last five minutes,
what was the temperature?

00:29:38.540 --> 00:29:40.370
Then I'm doing a very
simplistic check.

00:29:40.370 --> 00:29:44.210
If the temperature was over
x, send the message back

00:29:44.210 --> 00:29:45.560
into Pub/Sub.

00:29:45.560 --> 00:29:48.650
So here, Pub/Sub is not only
being used as the ingestion

00:29:48.650 --> 00:29:51.980
mechanism into Dataflow,
it's being used as the glue

00:29:51.980 --> 00:29:53.570
to connect systems together.

00:29:53.570 --> 00:29:56.100
So I'm sending a message
saying AC on or off.

00:29:56.100 --> 00:29:57.890
And then the factory
can pick that up.

00:29:57.890 --> 00:30:01.070
More interestingly,
down this middle path--

00:30:01.070 --> 00:30:03.897
that was a simple, is the
temperature over 30 degrees?

00:30:03.897 --> 00:30:05.480
But down this middle
path, we actually

00:30:05.480 --> 00:30:08.080
called the cloud machine
learning service.

00:30:08.080 --> 00:30:10.425
Here, we have
built a model based

00:30:10.425 --> 00:30:12.050
on data that would
have been previously

00:30:12.050 --> 00:30:14.000
recorded in BigQuery,
all that raw data

00:30:14.000 --> 00:30:15.590
that we should have recorded.

00:30:15.590 --> 00:30:19.730
It is able to then tell us
what the optimum time to turn

00:30:19.730 --> 00:30:20.990
the fans on.

00:30:20.990 --> 00:30:23.060
Here, instead of making
that simple check,

00:30:23.060 --> 00:30:27.710
I make a rest call to the
inference service on the model.

00:30:27.710 --> 00:30:29.960
It will tell me whether the
fan should come on based

00:30:29.960 --> 00:30:31.790
on the heuristics that we--

00:30:31.790 --> 00:30:35.000
based on the cost optimization
that we had in mind,

00:30:35.000 --> 00:30:36.320
which is efficiency.

00:30:36.320 --> 00:30:38.840
And it will then, again,
we put it back into Pub/Sub

00:30:38.840 --> 00:30:41.930
to push back into our factories.

00:30:41.930 --> 00:30:44.000
We're talking heavily
about retail use case

00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:49.550
here with the sales data
that William went through

00:30:49.550 --> 00:30:51.110
on Data Prep.

00:30:51.110 --> 00:30:52.550
A simple example
for retail would

00:30:52.550 --> 00:30:54.470
be take those IT
temperature readings

00:30:54.470 --> 00:30:56.540
and imagine they're
actually coming from stores.

00:30:56.540 --> 00:30:58.070
And what we're trying to
do is, what temperature

00:30:58.070 --> 00:30:59.690
should the store
be set at to make

00:30:59.690 --> 00:31:02.148
sure people are spending lots
of good money in your stores?

00:31:02.148 --> 00:31:02.684
OK.

00:31:02.684 --> 00:31:03.350
Back to William.

00:31:03.350 --> 00:31:05.683
WILLIAM VAMBENEPE: Can we go
back to the slides, please?

00:31:08.590 --> 00:31:10.930
We talked about the difficulty
of writing pipelines

00:31:10.930 --> 00:31:12.700
and how you can
now, in many cases,

00:31:12.700 --> 00:31:14.230
not even have to write any code.

00:31:14.230 --> 00:31:15.990
Or if you do have
to write some code,

00:31:15.990 --> 00:31:19.630
have a very powerful and
convenient programming model,

00:31:19.630 --> 00:31:22.450
like Beam, running on
Dataflow, which Reza showed.

00:31:22.450 --> 00:31:24.840
That really is only
the tip of the iceberg,

00:31:24.840 --> 00:31:27.400
though, in terms of
complexity usually faced,

00:31:27.400 --> 00:31:30.010
in terms of running big
data in an organization.

00:31:30.010 --> 00:31:33.280
Quite often, the hard part that
comes after that is, how do you

00:31:33.280 --> 00:31:34.220
operationalize that?

00:31:34.220 --> 00:31:34.720
OK.

00:31:34.720 --> 00:31:37.030
You've written your
first pipeline.

00:31:37.030 --> 00:31:40.860
How do you make sure
it runs at scale

00:31:40.860 --> 00:31:43.030
with just the right
amount of infrastructure?

00:31:43.030 --> 00:31:44.939
And that's often
pretty complicated.

00:31:44.939 --> 00:31:46.480
And there are two
main aspects there.

00:31:46.480 --> 00:31:47.910
One is the human aspect.

00:31:47.910 --> 00:31:50.650
It is a lot of work
for people to have

00:31:50.650 --> 00:31:53.500
to deal with
provisioning and patching

00:31:53.500 --> 00:31:57.340
and reliability and security
and performance and optimization

00:31:57.340 --> 00:31:59.874
and all those
operational aspects which

00:31:59.874 --> 00:32:00.790
you have to deal with.

00:32:00.790 --> 00:32:06.100
Because by definition, big data
runs on a significant amount

00:32:06.100 --> 00:32:07.640
of computing power.

00:32:07.640 --> 00:32:09.280
So you have to manage
it efficiently.

00:32:09.280 --> 00:32:11.250
Otherwise, you are
spending way too much.

00:32:11.250 --> 00:32:12.550
It's just not doable.

00:32:12.550 --> 00:32:14.140
And so in reality,
this is where you

00:32:14.140 --> 00:32:16.900
want to spend your time,
bringing your business

00:32:16.900 --> 00:32:20.380
expertise to the data and
not doing all the operations

00:32:20.380 --> 00:32:23.590
and trying to guess how much
capacity you're going to need.

00:32:23.590 --> 00:32:28.250
So there's a human workload
aspect and skill set

00:32:28.250 --> 00:32:30.800
set of constraints here.

00:32:30.800 --> 00:32:33.940
In addition to that,
there is just resources.

00:32:33.940 --> 00:32:35.800
How often have
you found yourself

00:32:35.800 --> 00:32:39.730
feeling like you have a tiny
shovel and a really big job?

00:32:39.730 --> 00:32:41.830
And you're not getting
the resources you need.

00:32:41.830 --> 00:32:43.390
But that's because
it's really hard

00:32:43.390 --> 00:32:45.200
to get large amount
of resources,

00:32:45.200 --> 00:32:47.320
at least until you
know for sure what

00:32:47.320 --> 00:32:49.030
you are trying to get done.

00:32:49.030 --> 00:32:51.100
And the important thing
is that you really

00:32:51.100 --> 00:32:53.410
want to encourage
people to try things,

00:32:53.410 --> 00:32:56.870
to iterate, to fail quickly,
to move on to the next thing.

00:32:56.870 --> 00:32:59.590
But if they cannot get
infrastructure to support this

00:32:59.590 --> 00:33:02.860
experimentation, they're
going to do toy projects.

00:33:02.860 --> 00:33:06.340
And the solution here is
what the industry now called

00:33:06.340 --> 00:33:11.380
serverless, which is an approach
to data processing that takes

00:33:11.380 --> 00:33:15.070
care of and automates all
of the management [? NME ?]

00:33:15.070 --> 00:33:17.751
[? session ?] processes and
allows you to focus on writing

00:33:17.751 --> 00:33:18.250
the job.

00:33:18.250 --> 00:33:23.210
So insight, business
expertise, no administration.

00:33:23.210 --> 00:33:26.650
It also means that you get
access to a lot of resources

00:33:26.650 --> 00:33:27.670
when you need them.

00:33:27.670 --> 00:33:30.190
But you only pay exactly
for what you use.

00:33:30.190 --> 00:33:32.680
And so if you have an
idea in the morning,

00:33:32.680 --> 00:33:36.370
you can start and analyze
large amounts of data

00:33:36.370 --> 00:33:38.920
to really validate your idea.

00:33:38.920 --> 00:33:44.170
And if it doesn't work, you've
wasted an hour and $5.00 worth

00:33:44.170 --> 00:33:45.840
of processing.

00:33:45.840 --> 00:33:47.770
If, on the other
hand, it does work,

00:33:47.770 --> 00:33:49.480
well, you don't
have a toy project

00:33:49.480 --> 00:33:53.530
that you have to throw away
and reimplement at large scale.

00:33:53.530 --> 00:33:56.410
You have something which
is built on services that

00:33:56.410 --> 00:33:58.000
can natively scale to any size.

00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:00.910
And so any experiment
that is successful

00:34:00.910 --> 00:34:04.270
is ready to operationalize
and productize right away.

00:34:04.270 --> 00:34:07.540
And that's really-- the
most important part is that.

00:34:07.540 --> 00:34:08.889
It's not the technology.

00:34:08.889 --> 00:34:13.420
It's really the way teams
learn to work and test

00:34:13.420 --> 00:34:16.000
ideas and iterate
very, very quickly,

00:34:16.000 --> 00:34:21.085
as opposed to any new idea
being yet another IT project.

00:34:21.085 --> 00:34:23.270
In practice, when I
talk about serverless,

00:34:23.270 --> 00:34:25.380
well, there's many
services on the platform.

00:34:25.380 --> 00:34:30.190
But the three that I would call
out as the most obvious example

00:34:30.190 --> 00:34:33.370
have the nice characteristic
that they cover the three

00:34:33.370 --> 00:34:35.889
main stages of data lifecycle.

00:34:35.889 --> 00:34:38.889
Reza talked about Pub/Sub
for event ingestion.

00:34:38.889 --> 00:34:40.929
Nothing to manage,
just [? query ?] topic

00:34:40.929 --> 00:34:42.760
and pump as many
events as you want.

00:34:42.760 --> 00:34:45.969
And only pay based on how
much data you put through.

00:34:45.969 --> 00:34:48.520
Don't have to guess and
pre-provision ahead of time.

00:34:48.520 --> 00:34:49.830
Dataflow, as well.

00:34:49.830 --> 00:34:54.250
Reza described the quality
of the programming model.

00:34:54.250 --> 00:34:56.350
But the thing that's really
unique about Dataflow

00:34:56.350 --> 00:34:59.740
as a [? Beam ?] runner is
it's a serverless system where

00:34:59.740 --> 00:35:01.050
you don't deploy the cluster.

00:35:01.050 --> 00:35:03.850
You submit a job and then
the job automatically

00:35:03.850 --> 00:35:06.280
gets the resources
it needs, autoscale.

00:35:06.280 --> 00:35:09.880
So whether you use Dataprep to
create that job or you write

00:35:09.880 --> 00:35:12.820
it yourself, once you've created
the pipeline, you're done.

00:35:12.820 --> 00:35:14.620
Everything else is automated.

00:35:14.620 --> 00:35:16.960
And then the third one, which
we are talking about now,

00:35:16.960 --> 00:35:19.270
is BigQuery for SQL analytics.

00:35:19.270 --> 00:35:21.000
Again, serverless
and at any scale.

00:35:21.000 --> 00:35:23.109
And so Reza, please,
tell us about BigQuery.

