00:36:39.890 --> 00:36:41.750
here and talk
about floppy disks.

00:36:41.750 --> 00:36:43.590
If you wanted to
store floppy disks,

00:36:43.590 --> 00:36:46.670
a petabyte onto floppy disks,
you'd have to stack up 12

00:36:46.670 --> 00:36:48.980
Empire State buildings'
worth of them.

00:36:48.980 --> 00:36:52.610
It would take you 27
years to download over 4G.

00:36:52.610 --> 00:36:56.870
And it's every tweet
ever made times 50.

00:36:56.870 --> 00:36:59.920
But that's a big number,
obviously, a petabyte.

00:36:59.920 --> 00:37:02.180
But it can also
be a small number.

00:37:02.180 --> 00:37:06.550
So It's the data that's encoded
in just two micrograms of DNA.

00:37:06.550 --> 00:37:09.170
It's one day's worth
of YouTube video.

00:37:09.170 --> 00:37:13.100
It's 200 servers logging,
at a fair pace, admittedly,

00:37:13.100 --> 00:37:15.157
50 entries a second
for three years.

00:37:15.157 --> 00:37:17.240
So there's going to be
more and more datasets that

00:37:17.240 --> 00:37:19.940
actually have these
kind of volumes of data

00:37:19.940 --> 00:37:21.800
as we move forward.

00:37:21.800 --> 00:37:24.340
And where did
BigQuery come from?

00:37:24.340 --> 00:37:26.750
BigQuery's based on a
technology called Dremel.

00:37:26.750 --> 00:37:31.700
And in 2002, Google had
more and more data to store

00:37:31.700 --> 00:37:33.410
and the Google File
System was bought.

00:37:33.410 --> 00:37:36.710
It allowed you to store all of
this data on commodity servers.

00:37:36.710 --> 00:37:37.280
Fantastic.

00:37:37.280 --> 00:37:39.680
We solved the problem of
how to store lots of data.

00:37:39.680 --> 00:37:42.904
By 2004, we had
lots of data and we

00:37:42.904 --> 00:37:44.570
had to figure out the
problem of, how do

00:37:44.570 --> 00:37:46.650
you process all of that data?

00:37:46.650 --> 00:37:49.940
So Jeff Dean and co.,
MapReduce was used

00:37:49.940 --> 00:37:52.610
and it was very heavily
used to actually

00:37:52.610 --> 00:37:55.820
be able to process all
of the data that we had.

00:37:55.820 --> 00:37:57.620
Now, when you're
doing analytics,

00:37:57.620 --> 00:38:00.489
you don't just write the
perfect query at the end.

00:38:00.489 --> 00:38:02.030
You wake up in the
morning and you've

00:38:02.030 --> 00:38:03.320
got it all ready for you.

00:38:03.320 --> 00:38:05.834
You will look at your data,
you want to analyze it.

00:38:05.834 --> 00:38:07.250
If you're doing a
MapReduce, you'd

00:38:07.250 --> 00:38:09.410
have to do some analysis
with the first MapReduce.

00:38:09.410 --> 00:38:11.451
Then you would take the
result set, look at that,

00:38:11.451 --> 00:38:12.530
do another MapReduce.

00:38:12.530 --> 00:38:16.160
And so our engineers got bored
with going out for coffee

00:38:16.160 --> 00:38:17.980
every time they ran
that first MapReduce

00:38:17.980 --> 00:38:20.600
and building all the plumbing
in between the stuff.

00:38:20.600 --> 00:38:21.710
And they wrote Dremel.

00:38:21.710 --> 00:38:23.390
And Dremel allows
you to run queries

00:38:23.390 --> 00:38:26.660
against petabytes of data, as
we're doing in the demo now.

00:38:26.660 --> 00:38:29.660
And it's the same
technology that has

00:38:29.660 --> 00:38:31.280
been externalized as BigQuery.

00:38:31.280 --> 00:38:35.480
So it's the same code base, it's
the same SREs and engineers.

00:38:35.480 --> 00:38:38.180
And within Google today,
Dremel is the core

00:38:38.180 --> 00:38:39.650
of our ad hoc analytics.

00:38:39.650 --> 00:38:41.660
And with their
warehousing needs,

00:38:41.660 --> 00:38:43.760
most Googlers will touch
it, myself and William

00:38:43.760 --> 00:38:46.520
will touch it more directly,
things with Dremel.

00:38:46.520 --> 00:38:52.180
However, other people will be
using it through dashboards.

00:38:52.180 --> 00:38:52.680
OK.

