00:39:35.600 --> 00:39:38.450
One of the other issues that's
been preventing or making

00:39:38.450 --> 00:39:41.030
it harder to access
and analyze data scale

00:39:41.030 --> 00:39:43.250
is the siloed
nature of the data.

00:39:43.250 --> 00:39:46.807
The fact that, quite often,
data is within a tool.

00:39:46.807 --> 00:39:48.890
And if you want to use
another tool to process it,

00:39:48.890 --> 00:39:52.600
well, then you have to
extract it, move it, copy it,

00:39:52.600 --> 00:39:54.070
have different versions.

00:39:54.070 --> 00:39:55.640
And at Google, we
have lots of ways

00:39:55.640 --> 00:39:59.030
to store data and lots of
ways to process data and lots

00:39:59.030 --> 00:40:00.320
of data between the two.

00:40:00.320 --> 00:40:02.180
And if we had to do
that, that would just

00:40:02.180 --> 00:40:03.800
explode the data centers.

00:40:03.800 --> 00:40:08.030
And so one fundamental
principle is that any tool

00:40:08.030 --> 00:40:10.851
can access data in any storage.

00:40:10.851 --> 00:40:12.350
And that's really,
really important,

00:40:12.350 --> 00:40:18.760
in terms of allowing a much
broader analysis across sources

00:40:18.760 --> 00:40:19.580
of datasets.

00:40:19.580 --> 00:40:23.720
And so in Cloud, the way
that [? matterizes ?]

00:40:23.720 --> 00:40:27.650
itself is with something like
Dataflow, which Reza described.

00:40:27.650 --> 00:40:31.290
Being able to access data
in Google Cloud storage,

00:40:31.290 --> 00:40:36.050
in BigQuery, in BigTable--
that's our NoSQL service--

00:40:36.050 --> 00:40:38.760
in data store, all the
databases that we have.

00:40:38.760 --> 00:40:40.520
In Pub/Sub for event stream.

00:40:40.520 --> 00:40:44.960
Wherever data is, Dataflow
can get it and process it.

00:40:44.960 --> 00:40:47.750
Now, it's maybe less
intuitive, but the same thing

00:40:47.750 --> 00:40:50.240
is true for BigQuery, as well.

00:40:50.240 --> 00:40:52.772
If you're going to try to go
through a petabyte of data

00:40:52.772 --> 00:40:53.480
in-- what was it?

00:40:53.480 --> 00:40:55.250
Two minutes and 40 seconds?--

00:40:55.250 --> 00:40:57.560
you have to put the
data in BigQuery storage

00:40:57.560 --> 00:41:01.160
because it's a highly optimized
storage system for analytics.

00:41:01.160 --> 00:41:03.680
But in many cases, you don't
have petabytes of data.

00:41:03.680 --> 00:41:07.190
It might be more convenient
to leave the data elsewhere

00:41:07.190 --> 00:41:11.180
as a file in Google
Cloud Storage in BigTable

00:41:11.180 --> 00:41:13.280
or maybe just even
in Google Drive.

00:41:13.280 --> 00:41:17.600
So in my laptop, I can
have a CSV file, which I

00:41:17.600 --> 00:41:19.880
drag in my Google Drive folder.

00:41:19.880 --> 00:41:21.730
It gets synchronized
to Google Drive

00:41:21.730 --> 00:41:24.200
and now I can write
a BigQuery query that

00:41:24.200 --> 00:41:27.740
is going to join from that
CSV file in Google Drive,

00:41:27.740 --> 00:41:31.160
with a log table in BigQuery.

00:41:31.160 --> 00:41:33.950
And so BigQuery has
that functionality

00:41:33.950 --> 00:41:38.080
to bring SQL analytics to
all the structured data

00:41:38.080 --> 00:41:39.500
on the Cloud.

00:41:39.500 --> 00:41:41.000
And then finally,
another product

00:41:41.000 --> 00:41:44.030
we haven't talked about yet
is Dataproc for [? managed ?]

00:41:44.030 --> 00:41:45.470
Spark and Hadoop.

00:41:45.470 --> 00:41:48.260
What it allows you to do is to
bring the power and richness

00:41:48.260 --> 00:41:51.860
of the Hadoop ecosystem
and Spark ecosystem

00:41:51.860 --> 00:41:52.880
to Google Cloud.

00:41:52.880 --> 00:41:56.280
Because the key thing you use
the right tool for the job.

00:41:56.280 --> 00:42:00.110
And if the right tool is a Spark
library that somebody wrote,

00:42:00.110 --> 00:42:01.810
then you should be
able to use that.

00:42:01.810 --> 00:42:05.570
And Dataproc will give you
a fully configured cluster

00:42:05.570 --> 00:42:07.730
in about 90 seconds.

00:42:07.730 --> 00:42:10.520
And that cluster will
have direct access

00:42:10.520 --> 00:42:13.400
to data, again, in the
storage system, such

00:42:13.400 --> 00:42:17.000
that the first thing you do is
not load the data in Hadoop.

00:42:17.000 --> 00:42:19.100
The first thing you
do is run the job.

00:42:19.100 --> 00:42:22.150
So you can start a
job, run, shut down

00:42:22.150 --> 00:42:25.160
your cluster when you're done,
and you pay by the minute.

00:42:25.160 --> 00:42:27.855
And so it's standard
Hadoop and Spark,

00:42:27.855 --> 00:42:31.730
but it enables them to run in
a very dynamic, efficient Cloud

00:42:31.730 --> 00:42:32.570
native way.

00:42:32.570 --> 00:42:36.300
And again, accessing
your data wherever it is.

00:42:36.300 --> 00:42:39.260
And then finally, the last set
of issues that people typically

00:42:39.260 --> 00:42:41.360
run into is just
the tooling to do

00:42:41.360 --> 00:42:42.710
something useful with the data.

00:42:42.710 --> 00:42:44.420
Because at the end,
it's nice to be

00:42:44.420 --> 00:42:47.870
able to collect it and
process it, but to what end?

00:42:47.870 --> 00:42:50.960
And going back to the use
cases, quite often, that end

00:42:50.960 --> 00:42:53.520
is to inform a human.

00:42:53.520 --> 00:42:55.680
So there are tools
that help you do that.

00:42:55.680 --> 00:42:57.180
On the platform,
specifically, there

00:42:57.180 --> 00:43:01.160
are two main tools which
are directly accessible.

00:43:01.160 --> 00:43:02.310
One is Datalab.

00:43:02.310 --> 00:43:05.390
Datalab is targeted
at data scientists.

00:43:05.390 --> 00:43:08.270
And so for data scientists,
Jupiter notebooks

00:43:08.270 --> 00:43:11.420
are a very popular
way to analyze data.

00:43:11.420 --> 00:43:16.370
And what Datalab is is Jupiter
closely integrated with Google

00:43:16.370 --> 00:43:18.830
Cloud platform to
allow data scientists

00:43:18.830 --> 00:43:22.620
to benefit from all the
Jupiter modules and experience,

00:43:22.620 --> 00:43:25.850
but do that in a way that
enables collaboration

00:43:25.850 --> 00:43:27.312
and usage on the platform.

00:43:27.312 --> 00:43:29.020
And Reza is going to
show that in action.

00:43:29.020 --> 00:43:30.039
REZA ROKNI: Yeah.

00:43:30.039 --> 00:43:31.580
The interesting
thing about notebooks

00:43:31.580 --> 00:43:35.330
is it's the data
scientists' scratchpad.

00:43:35.330 --> 00:43:37.580
As they're going through
and analyzing the data,

00:43:37.580 --> 00:43:39.570
they can just record
their results.

00:43:39.570 --> 00:43:40.839
They can go back to them.

00:43:40.839 --> 00:43:42.380
They can use that,
even, as something

00:43:42.380 --> 00:43:44.400
that you pass to the
data engineers, which

00:43:44.400 --> 00:43:47.240
would be me, to actually
go and put into production.

00:43:47.240 --> 00:43:48.170
And the other thing--

00:43:48.170 --> 00:43:49.190
the great way that
I've seen them

00:43:49.190 --> 00:43:50.990
used is, at the end
of their conclusions,

00:43:50.990 --> 00:43:53.790
after they've come to what they
think is the right way forward,

00:43:53.790 --> 00:43:56.122
they'll present the notebook
back to their colleagues.

00:43:56.122 --> 00:43:57.830
And that's great
because at the end, when

00:43:57.830 --> 00:43:59.570
they have that final
result, you might

00:43:59.570 --> 00:44:02.634
have a question like, why did
you use column A not column B?

00:44:02.634 --> 00:44:04.050
And they'll go
back and say, well,

00:44:04.050 --> 00:44:06.870
actually, I looked at column A
and I saw a skew in the data.

00:44:06.870 --> 00:44:08.370
Therefore, I
switched to column B.

00:44:08.370 --> 00:44:11.310
And it's a great way to actually
maintain all of the knowledge

00:44:11.310 --> 00:44:13.650
that you gain as you
explore your dataset.

00:44:13.650 --> 00:44:16.410
If we could switch to
the laptop, please.

